ratap Singh of Idar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Sir Pratap Singh was a Rathore prince of Jodhpur, long its chief minister and regent, who became Maharaja of Idar from 1902 to 1911.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-jammu-and-kashmir"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pratap-singh-of-jammu-and-kashmir.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Singh of Jammu and Kashmir</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Singh was the Dogra Maharaja of Jammu and Kashmir from 1885 to 1925.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-of-satara"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pratap-singh-of-satara.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Singh of Satara</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Singh was a descendant of Shivaji and nominal Chhatrapati of the Marathas under the Peshwas.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singh-prabhakar"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pratap-singh-prabhakar.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Singh Prabhakar</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Singh Prabhakar was a Naruka Kachhwaha chief who broke away from Jaipur in the 1770s and founded the state of Alwar in north-eastern Rajasthan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratap-singha"><span class="enc-name">Pratap Singha</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratap Singha, or Susenghphaa, was king of the Ahom kingdom of Assam from 1603 to 1641.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/prataparudra-kakatiya"><span class="enc-name">Prataparudra (Kakatiya dynasty)</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Prataparudra was the last ruler of the Kakatiya dynasty, reigning from Warangal from 1289 to 1323 as the grandson and successor of Rudramadevi.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prataparudra-deva"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/prataparudra-deva.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prataparudra Deva</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Prataparudra Deva was the last great ruler of the Gajapati Kingdom of Odisha.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratapgad"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pratapgad.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pratapgad</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratapgad is a hill fort in the Satara district of Maharashtra, western India, built in 1656 on the orders of Shivaji to guard the passes of the Western Ghats.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratapsinh-gaekwad"><span class="enc-name">Pratapsinh Gaekwad</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratapsinh Gaekwad was the last ruling Maharaja of Baroda, succeeding his grandfather Sayajirao Gaekwad III in 1939.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pratinas"><span class="enc-name">Pratinas</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pratinas of Phlius was a Greek dramatist and lyric poet active in Athens around 500 BCE, when he competed against Aeschylus.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prato-cathedral"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/prato-cathedral.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prato Cathedral</span><span class="enc-type">Work / monument</span><span class="enc-snip">Prato Cathedral, dedicated to Santo Stefano, grew from the parish church around which the town developed and was enlarged from the 12th to the 15th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/prattigau"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/prattigau.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Prättigau</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">The Prättigau is an Alpine valley of the Landquart river in the canton of Graubünden, eastern Switzerland.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravahana-jaivali"><span class="enc-name">Pravahana Jaivali</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravahana Jaivali was a king of Panchala who appears in the Chandogya and Brihadaranyaka Upanishads.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravarapura"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pravarapura.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pravarapura</span><span class="enc-type">Place</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravarapura was a capital of the main branch of the Vakataka dynasty in central India, founded by King Pravarasena II in the 5th century.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-i"><span class="enc-name">Pravarasena I</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravarasena I was the son of Vindhyashakti and the second ruler of the Vakataka dynasty of the Deccan.</span></a></li><li class="enc-item has-img"><a href="/encyclopedia/pravarasena-ii"><img class="enc-thumb" src="/images/encyclopedia/thumbs/pravarasena-ii.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="56" height="56"><span class="enc-name">Pravarasena II</span><span class="enc-type">Person</span><span class="enc-snip">Pravarasena II was a 5th-century king of the main branch of the Vakataka dynasty in the Deccan, son of Rudrasena II and the Gupta princess Prabhavatigupta.</span></a></li></ul>
